    <h2>1. The Geotechnical Failure Wedge (Rankine Theory)</h2>
    <p>
      In geotechnical engineering, the soil directly behind a retaining wall does not push as a solid block. Instead, it shears along an internal slip plane known as the <strong>Rankine Active Failure Wedge</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The angle of this slip plane (&theta;) is calculated from the soil's internal friction angle (&phi;, typically 30 degrees for average sandy/gravelly loam):
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      Failure Wedge Angle (&theta;) = 45° + (&phi; / 2) = 45° + (30° / 2) = 60° from horizontal
    </div>
    <p>
      This means that for a 4-foot high retaining wall, the triangular wedge of soil trying to slide forward and push the wall over extends <strong>2.3 feet back from the base</strong> at the top of the wall.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Cardinal Rule of Deadman Anchors:</strong> A deadman timber that is only 2 feet long sits entirely INSIDE the sliding failure wedge! When the wedge slides forward, the short deadman moves forward WITH the soil, providing ZERO anchoring power. To function, the deadman anchor must extend <strong>at least 4 to 6 feet back into the hillside</strong>, anchoring firmly into the undisturbed, stable native soil outside the failure zone.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Anatomy of a Heavy-Duty Timber Deadman Anchor</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Component</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Dimensions &amp; Material</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Structural Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">1. Tieback Stem (Trunk)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">6x6 Timber (4 ft to 6 ft Long)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Carries pure horizontal tension from the wall face back to the anchor</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">2. Crossbar (T-Head)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">6x6 Timber (3 ft to 4 ft Long)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Mobilizes passive soil resistance; acts like a subterranean boat anchor</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">3. Half-Lap Face Joint</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Notched 2-3/4" deep into wall course</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Locks the stem flush into the wall without bulging the exterior profile</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">4. Ground Pinning Stakes</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Two 36" x 1/2" #4 Steel Rebar Pins</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Pins the T-head into native soil so compaction cannot shift it during backfill</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Deadman Sizing &amp; Spacing Rules by Wall Height</h2>
    <p>
      The number and length of deadman anchors required escalates rapidly as wall height increases, because lateral soil pressure increases with the square of the height (H&sup2;):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Under 3 Feet High:</strong> Deadman anchors are optional if the wall has a 3/4" per foot inward batter and the base course is buried 6 inches.</li>
      <li><strong>3 to 4 Feet High:</strong> Install 1 row of deadman anchors at course 3 or 4 (approximately mid-height), spaced every <strong>6 to 8 feet horizontally</strong> with 4-foot stems.</li>
      <li><strong>4 to 6 Feet High:</strong> Install 2 staggered rows of deadman anchors: Row 1 at course 3, and Row 2 at course 6. Stems must extend <strong>6 feet deep into the slope</strong> spaced every 6 feet on-center.</li>
      <li><strong>Over 6 Feet High:</strong> Timber walls over 6 feet are generally not recommended for DIY construction; local building codes mandate engineered geogrid-reinforced segmental block walls designed by a licensed PE.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Step-by-Step Deadman Installation Technique</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Excavate the Trench:</strong> When your wall reaches course 3 or 4, dig a perpendicular horizontal trench 12 inches wide and 4 to 5 feet back into the hillside.</li>
      <li><strong>Notch the Front Course:</strong> Cut a 5-1/2" wide by 2-3/4" deep notch in the front wall course where the stem will exit, or cut half-laps on both the wall timber and the deadman stem.</li>
      <li><strong>Fasten the T-Head Crossbar:</strong> Position the 3-foot crossbar perpendicular at the rear of the stem. Fasten it with two 10-inch structural timber screws or two 12-inch rebar spikes driven through pre-drilled holes.</li>
      <li><strong>Drive the Anchor Stakes:</strong> Drill two 1/2" holes through the crossbar into the earth. Use a 4-lb sledgehammer to drive 36-inch lengths of #4 steel rebar straight down into the subgrade.</li>
      <li><strong>Backfill and Compact:</strong> Pack crushed stone or well-graded structural gravel around the T-head and compact in 6-inch lifts. The weight of the compacted soil bearing down on the deadman permanently locks the wall in place.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    expertName="David Miller, PE"
    role="Senior Structural & Geotechnical Engineer"
    experience="25+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Installing a Deadman on Top of Crushed Drainage Stone is a Fatal Error"
    note="A critical geotechnical error I inspect involves homeowners who excavate their drainage gravel trench, fill it with crushed stone, and then lay their deadman timber right in the gravel bed. A deadman relies on PASSIVE EARTH RESISTANCE—the soil in front of the buried crossbar must be solid, dense, undisturbed native earth that resists being pulled forward. If you excavate a massive trench and backfill the entire area around the crossbar with loose gravel without mechanical compaction, the crossbar has nothing solid to pull against! When the wall experiences lateral load, the deadman simply pulls through the loose rock like a spoon through sugar. Always dig a narrow, tight trench for the stem, and cut the crossbar slot into undisturbed, virgin bank soil."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Deadman Tieback Installation"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Cutting the Deadman Stem Too Short (Sitting in Active Failure Zone)",
        costImpact: "$3,600 (Wall Tilts Forward & Complete Reconstruction Required)",
        description: "Installing a 2-foot or 3-foot deadman leaves the anchor inside the sliding shear wedge. As the soil slides, the anchor travels with it, providing zero resistance.",
        solution: "Extend deadman stems at least 4 to 6 feet back into the slope so the anchor crossbar bites solidly into undisturbed native earth beyond the failure plane."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Omitting the Perpendicular T-Head Crossbar (Using Plain 6x6 Stems)",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (Frictional Pullout Failure Under Saturated Soil Loads)",
        description: "A plain 6x6 timber without a crossbar relies purely on skin friction along its smooth sides. Under heavy hydrostatic loads, the straight beam pulls clean out of the soil.",
        solution: "Always fasten a 36-inch to 48-inch perpendicular crossbar to the end of the stem to mobilize a wide trapezoidal plug of passive soil resistance."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Fastening Deadman with Regular Drywall or Deck Screws",
        costImpact: "$1,200 (Sheared Screws & Wall Face Disconnection)",
        description: "Lightweight 3\" wood screws shear off under several thousand pounds of horizontal tension, separating the wall face from the buried anchor.",
        solution: "Connect the deadman to the wall face using half-lap joints pinned with 1/2\" x 12\" steel rebar spikes or heavy 5/16\" structural timber screws."
      }
    ]}
  />

